          item: "https://www.thehonestreviewers.com/best-of/",
        },
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 3,
          name: "Can a Portable Power Station Run a CPAP",
          item: "https://www.thehonestreviewers.com/can-a-portable-power-station-run-a-cpap/",
        },
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "FAQPage",
      mainEntity: [
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Can a portable power station run a CPAP machine?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "Yes, and it is one of the loads power stations handle best. A CPAP without heated humidification draws only 30 to 60 watts, so even a small 500Wh unit typically covers two nights. Use a pure sine wave unit, and check your machine's own manual for any battery guidance.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "How many nights will a power station run a CPAP?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "With the humidifier off, roughly one night per 300 to 400Wh of usable capacity. A 500Wh unit gives one to two nights, 1,000Wh gives two to three, and 2,000Wh gives four or five. Turning the heated humidifier on cuts every one of those figures by half or more.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Does the humidifier use a lot of power?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "Enormously, in relative terms. Heating water is the single largest draw in the system and can take a machine from 40 watts to well over 100. Switching off heated humidification and the heated hose is the most effective thing you can do to extend runtime, often doubling or tripling it.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Should I run a CPAP on AC or DC from a power station?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "DC if your machine supports it, using the manufacturer's own DC cord into the unit's 12V output. That bypasses the inverter and typically saves ten to twenty per cent of the energy, which is often the difference between two nights and three.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Do I need a pure sine wave power station for a CPAP?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "Yes. Many CPAP machines will not run correctly on modified sine wave, and manufacturers commonly advise against it. Since this is medical equipment running unattended while you sleep, it is not a place to accept an unknown or approximate waveform.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Can I use a power station for CPAP while camping?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "It is one of the most popular uses for these units. Turn off humidification, run on DC, and pair the unit with a solar panel if you are out for more than a couple of nights. A 500 to 1,000Wh unit with a 100W panel is a common and reliable setup.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Will a power station switch over fast enough if the power fails?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "Units with a UPS or pass-through mode switch in roughly ten to thirty milliseconds, which a CPAP will not notice. Without that feature the machine simply restarts when you plug it in, which usually wakes you. If uninterrupted therapy matters, look specifically for UPS or EPS mode.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "How do I work out my own CPAP's power use?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "Plug it into an energy meter and use it for one normal night at your usual pressure and settings. The kilowatt-hour reading is your figure. Pressure, mask leak and humidification all change consumption, so a measurement of your setup beats any published average.",
          },
        },
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "Article",
      headline: title,
      description: description,
      author: authorSchema(),
      datePublished: dates.published,
      dateModified: dates.modified,
      publisher: { "@type": "Organization", name: "The Honest Reviewers" },
